 or B3-B4-B5. Only B3-B4-B5 is possible. So B3 helps B3-B4-B5. Dangerous.\n- **B8**: completes B6-B7-B8 (B7:1 left), B7-B8-B9 (B7:1, B9:1). Low probability. Safer.\n- **T4**: completes T2-T3-T4 (T2:2, T3:2), T3-T4-T5 (T3:2, T5:4), T4-T5-T6. Several possibilities, but T-suit less active. No T melds visible. Relatively safe.\n- **T4 vs B8**: T4 has more combinations, but B8 requires rare B7/B9.\n\nB7 has 1 unshown, B9 has 1 \u2014 likely already used. So B6-B7-B8 unlikely.\n\nSimilarly, B8-B9-B1? No.\n\nSo B8 is safer than B3/B4/B5.\n\nT4: T2,T3,T5,T6 all available. T4 is more useful.\n\nSo **B8 is safest discard**.\n\nAlso, we have only one B8, no pair, no chow potential.\n\nWhereas B3/B4/B5 form a near-chow.\n\nSo preserve B3-B4-B5 for now.\n\nThus, **Play B8** is best.\n\nBut is there a chance for **Seven Pairs**? No \u2014 we have triples and melds.\n\nOr **All Simples** \u2014 yes, everything is simples.\n\nBut we need 8 points minimum.\n\nCurrent possible patterns:\n- All Simples (2 pts)\n- One Voided Suit? We have W, T, B \u2014 three suits \u2192 no.\n- No Honors? Yes \u2014 no honor tiles in hand or melds \u2192 **No Honors (1 pt)**\n- All Chows? We have one pung \u2192 no.\n- Mixed Double Chow? Not yet.\n\nBut if we complete B3-B4-B5 as chow, and have T4-T4 as pair, and keep W6, then hand would be:\n- Melds: [W3x3], [W5-W6-W7], [B3-B4-B5], and [T4-T4] pair? But need 14 tiles.\n\nWait: total tiles:\n- Melds: 3 + 3 = 6 tiles\n- Hand: 7 tiles \u2192 total 13, need 14th.\n\nAfter forming third meld, we\u2019d have 4 melds and a pair.\n\nBut we are not declaring chow yet \u2014 we are just holding.\n\nFor scoring, we need to win with valid hand.\n\nBut currently, no high-point pattern in sight.\n\nAlternative: **Pure Straight** (16 pts) \u2014 need 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in same suit, plus a fourth meld and pair.\n\nWe have W3, W5-W6-W7 \u2014 not helpful.\n\nBut if we go for **Bamboo Pure Straight**:\n- Need B1-B2-B3, B4-B5-B6, B7-B8-B9\nWe have B3,B4,B5,B8 \u2014 close, but B2=0, B1=4, B6=4, B7=1, B9=1\n\nB2 is gone \u2014 cannot form B1-B2-B3. Impossible.\n\nSo not feasible.\n\nThus, best to minimize risk and preserve flexibility.\n\nDiscard **B8** \u2014 safest tile.\n\nReason: B8 is part of no viable chow due to scarcity of B7 and B9 (only one each unshown, likely dead). It poses minimal danger and does not contribute to any strong potential meld in our hand. Other options like B3, B4, B5, and T4 are more central and dangerous. Preserving the B3-B4-B5 sequence offers potential for a chow and contributes to patterns like All Simples or Mixed Triple Chow. W6 is a Character tile, valuable for staying in suit for a possible Half or Full Flush attempt.\n\nAnswer: Play B8"}
