 useless.\n\nPerhaps discard W6, because I already have a W6 in the chow, so another player might be waiting for a specific wait.\n\nBut no.\n\nAfter careful evaluation, there is no significant difference.\n\nBut to comply with the format, I must choose.\n\nI'll go with discarding T8, as it is higher number and might be closer to edge, but not really.\n\nWait: **Lower Tiles** or **Upper Tiles** \u2014 but I have mixed.\n\nNo.\n\nAnswer: Play T8\n\nBut let's double-check the hand.\n\nWait: my hand is [W6: 1; T8: 1] \u2014 so one W6, one T8.\n\nIf I discard T8, I keep W6.\n\nThen I am waiting for W6.\n\nW6 has 2 left \u2014 possible.\n\nIf I discard W6, waiting for T8 \u2014 3 left.\n\nSo T8 is more likely to appear.\n\nTherefore, better to discard W6 and wait for T8, since T8 has higher probability (3 outs vs 2).\n\nYes.\n\nSo I should discard W6, keep T8.\n\nBecause there are more T8 tiles available, increasing my chances to draw or claim one.\n\nEven though I may not have enough fans, I should maximize win opportunity.\n\nSo Evaluation: Between discarding W6 or T8, keeping T8 offers a higher probability of completion since three T8 tiles remain unseen, compared to two W6 tiles. Although the hand likely lacks sufficient scoring patterns to reach 8 points, maximizing the chance to complete the hand is still preferable.\n\nReason: T8 has more remaining copies (3) than W6 (2), making it statistically more likely to acquire.\n\nAnswer: Play W6"}
